WEBVTT

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:36.400
Welcome.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.260
Howdy.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.290
Now, ain't that handy.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:41.470
How?

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.690
The way the road takes a bend in here, runs right by the front door.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.980
No, Mr. Clamber, this is your driveway.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.080
It goes with the house.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.260
This thing a hire?

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.020
Oh, yes, indeed.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.400
It was built by John Barrymore, one of our finest actors.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.460
Pretty fair stonemason, too.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:09.150
is here is dandy soil fine granny we'll commence flower tomorrow but this is beverly hill dirt is

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:23.390
dirt yes i know but why don't we look around inside here we are i hope you're going to like

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:30.000
this place i picked out for you trifle larger than what you're used to but i feel a man with 25

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.870
million dollars in my bank should live in a manner that come in come in this is your home

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:52.900
i say it may be a bit more than you're used to but i'm sure you'll be comfortable

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.620
now my secretary will be over right away to help you with the hiring of servants or whatever

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.000
problems you may have she's a very efficient girl this

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:13.820
Jed, Bethel fetch in my stove so I can get some vittles to cooking.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.540
Oh, you have a beautiful stove.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.120
Thank you.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.160
Fetch it in, Jed.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.270
You scare up some wood, Ellie.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.330
No, no, I mean you have a stove already installed in the kitchen.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.210
Where's that?

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:26.530
I'll show you.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.870
Ellie, you go with Granny, she'll still need you to fetch some wood.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:34.960
Sure, Pop.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:43.000
Uncle Jed, there's a whole 'nother house up here.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.810
She'll come down from there, like it's not that belonged to somebody else.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.270
You alright?

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:58.200
This is your kitchen, and here's the store.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.140
Word in food preparation.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.200
And speaking of food dough,

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.710
you'll find everything you could possibly want

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.430
stored right in here, see?

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.490
Well, I'll leave you ladies to your culinary delights.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:20.970
Au revoir.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.880
Bon appétit.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.040
Talks gibberish.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.990
You've sent a great deal of stowing this year.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.000
Stowed, Grant.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:44.560
we'll see. Ellie, out and fetch some wood. I'll get a fire going in here and we'll see.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.560
Metro, I hope it's all right for you to be missing school. Oh, sure, Uncle Jet. They

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.160
ain't straight in the fifth grade. You're in the fifth grade already? Starting this

00:03:55.160 --> 00:04:00.000
year. Don't seem like no time at all since I went with your ma to

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.280
get you started in the first grade.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.570
You was six years old.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.100
No bigger than that.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.500
We drove clean over to Oxford

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.290
so you could go to the same school your pa went to.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.580
You wouldn't know that town today, Uncle Jed.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:15.520
Oxford?

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:16.650
Yes, sir.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.120
Well, that town has grown and grown

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.310
and spread out and built up

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.390
and just kept getting bigger and bigger.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.710
Well, today, well, I bet you there must be

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.210
at least three or four dozen people living there.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.000
Hey, sir.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.850
That's all right to visit, but I wouldn't want to live there

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.100
Do you have any assistance?

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.950
Thank you, Mr. Drysdale

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.570
We were just talking about Jethro here getting back to school

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:44.630
Oh, well, we have some excellent educational facilities out here, Mr. Crabbit

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.470
Where do you go to school, Jethro?

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:47.430
Oxford

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.100
Oxford

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.860
Yeah, you see, Jethro's ma, that's my cousin Pearl

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.200
She married an Oxford man, so naturally Jethro, he goes to school there too

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.320
I'm the champion crawdad eater

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:07.800
oxford drill put away three buckets of crawdad four and he's learned to cipher and everything

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:17.930
cypher some for mr dry deal one and one's two two and two's four four and four is eight

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.260
i have to take off my shoes to get any problem i'm sure we're thinking of two different oxford

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.000
you see this one is very very famous and very old that's where jethro goes

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.840
It's a school. Been there since pioneer days.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.560
That's how come it got its name.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.030
It's where the oxen used to forward the reverence.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.010
So the folks took to calling it oxen forward

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.050
and later on got shortened to oxen.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:43.510
Well, I'll be running.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.030
Oh, by the way, here are your keys.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.260
My secretary should be over very soon.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.920
Well, thank you very much, Mr. Drysdale,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.360
for everything you've been doing for us.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.620
Oh, it's my pleasure, Mr. Clavitt.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.220
You see, I'm not only your banker,

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.140
I'm your next-door neighbor.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.000
Well, you say, how ain't that nice?

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:05.240
Well, now, why don't you bring your missus over tonight for supper, and I'll have Granny

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.540
womp up a mess of grits and hog jowls.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.150
Well, you see, right now my wife is in Boston.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:12.680
Well, that's too bad.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:13.300
When should she be back?

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.340
Not too soon, fortunately.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:16.940
I'll see you later, huh?

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.550
Well, thank you, Mr. Drydale.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:19.470
So long.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:27.220
Uncle Jed, you reckon one of these days I can have me a little bitty old taste of Granny's

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:27.740
moonshot?

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:28.980
I just wrote.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:30.000
You know the.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.380
family rule about granny's moonshine you get your first taste on your wedding day and not before

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:41.340
well uncle jed that might be an awful long time i ain't even got me no sweetheart how come why

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.720
back home every time i get me a girl one of them they're big fellas that take her right away from

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:52.800
me maybe out here in beverly hills paul could i please have the axe i gotta split some wood for

00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:00.000
granny. Bet you, Jethro. Ain't this place got a wood pile? Not that I can find, but I found a whole

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:04.160
A black old dead tree trunk, pretty near as high as this height.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:05.960
Is it kind of big for you to tackle?

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.960
Well, shucks, no, Paul, they're only this big around.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.540
And all trimmed and topped, and strung together with the black rope,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.140
so as no matter which one you cut, the others keep it from falling.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.720
Somebody had a right smart idea then.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.920
Just the same, I think I'd rather have Gethro do the cutting.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.680
Oh, Paul, and me?

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.000
Now, cutting trees and chopping logs ain't no work for a girl.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.240
I'm just as strong as Jethro.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:33.520
You ain't neither.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.060
I am so?

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.190
You ain't neither.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.210
I am so, too.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:38.290
You want to wrestle?

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:39.050
Don, too.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.310
Now, here, here.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:42.810
That ain't no fair fight.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.140
Somebody's gonna get hurt.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:45.880
Now, yeah, you see?

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:46.900
You're getting too rough.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:48.110
Get thrown.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:51.470
A person can't get crippled from a hole like that.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.230
Get up from there.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:54.070
I can't, Uncle Jeff.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:56.720
You turn him loose.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.640
Get up from there before I take the strap to the boot.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.210
Now here, you can get out there and chop down one of them tree trunks

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.370
Saw a granny, some nice logs

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:08.410
Yes, sir

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:14.620
Could've whooped you if you hadn't tripped me

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.130
Come back here and sit down, I want to talk to you

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.930
Then fire him square and I'll do it again

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.270
Uh, you won't

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:26.090
That's what I want to talk to you about

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:27.060
Ellie Mae?

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.980
too big to wrestle with boys.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:33.900
I ain't as big as Jethro.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.280
Big that way, I mean

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:37.960
growed up.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.680
You're a young lady now. You gotta start

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.010
minding your manners, fixing

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.660
yourself up real nice, and

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.680
wearing dresses. Paul,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.900
folks would call me a sissy.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:50.940
Well, then you're sissy for girls

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.240
to act like girls. You see, Ellie,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.710
I raised you like a boy

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.150
and I was wrong to do it.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.000
I reckon every man liked to have a son

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:08.720
young and the ma passed away i decided to turn you into a boy by the time granny come to help out

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:15.660
you was too wild to tame by thunder you could out run out climb out fight and out shoot every boy in

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:25.840
them hills still can pa but fit me it ain't right for folks to go against nature i'll look at old

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.000
duke reckon we could turn him into a cat of course not

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:40.940
that's right nature made him into a dog same way nature made you into a girl and lately she's

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:48.180
getting more and more positive about it you mean my years is grown no nothing like that

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:56.310
you're pretty oh pa i know you don't like it when i say that but you like it when the

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.000
young fellas around here come in saying it and they will only they'll probably be

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.230
be using words, fancy words I won't even understand. But Ellie Mae, ain't nobody can

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:11.670
ever tell you how pretty you really are simply. You're the living picture of your mom.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.180
You still here? I thought you were going up to the Clampett Estate.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.980
I didn't think of Putin for us both to be absent simultaneously. In the event of crises...

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.580
The only crises you have to worry about right now is keeping Mr. J. D. Clampett happy. Now get up there.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.120
Tell me, how did he like the flamingos?

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:48.050
What flamingos?

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:51.750
I thought pink flamingos around the swimming pool would add a rather elegant touch.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:56.030
Mr. Clampett is not a man educated to elegance. That will take time.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:58.090
Right now, there are more pressing problems.

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.000
Of course, getting settled, the servants...

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:04.070
problem. Well, whatever they are, just get up there and solve them. Now, J.D. Clampett is this

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:09.520
bank's largest depositor, and I'm making his satisfaction your responsibility. I accept the

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.660
Medal of Responsibility with which you have cloaked my shoulders, and I shall so conduct myself that

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:21.600
if this great financial institution shall last a thousand years, this was their finest hour.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:30.000
Andy Wood, all right. Got a lot of pitch and tar in it. Oughta burn real good. Yeah.

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.280
flimsy grate holes up.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:33.900
Granny!

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:37.200
Granny, this is the top of that tree trunk.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:38.840
The one I should split it up to.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:41.380
No, just leave it outside.

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:42.200
Yes, sir.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.260
This place ain't even got a wood box.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.800
Yeah, folks don't need much wood out here.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:48.760
Remember what Pearl said?

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:49.920
It don't get cold.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.600
Yeah, I remember.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:53.790
It might not get cold in the day,

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:56.370
but it sure freezes solid at night.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.060
How you know?

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:58.890
I show you how I know.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:29.410
bit of food in this here storage bin is frozen. It's harder than a rock. Yes. Skin hog. Dog.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:35.600
Take me two days to thaw out that ham for I can ever cook it.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:44.070
People will know better than store food up against the north wall.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.560
Tomorrow we'll dig us a root cellar and we'll put all this stuff in it.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:48.900
Get through.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.400
Go on out there and catch us a chicken.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.590
Okay Uncle Santa.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.730
How you know we got chickens?

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:56.930
At least this size is bound to have chickens.

00:12:56.930 --> 00:13:00.000
It ain't all frozen.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:10.820
Chick, chick, chick.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:30.500
A chicken?

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:33.270
Hey, hey.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.920
Bucket, you want it?

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:37.070
You take it, Ellie.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:38.310
See if you can find a creek.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:39.910
I gotta have some water.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:41.530
You want a pump on this, please?

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:43.270
It ain't even got a whale.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:44.890
Uncle Jed, Uncle Jed!

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.510
Yeah, boy, did you find a chicken?

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:47.510
I found something.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:50.850
I ain't sure if it's a chicken, a goose, a duck, or what.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:51.870
Where is it?

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.020
I couldn't catch it.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.070
That thing can outrun a jackrabbit.

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:57.250
No goose or duck.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:58.630
A chicken?

00:13:58.960 --> 00:13:59.860
If it is...

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.070
I was right about things growing bigger out here.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.700
The thing was this high.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.560
I got you through.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:07.550
Honest.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:09.330
It's got legs this long.

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:11.130
Dibby's on the drumstick.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.370
Drumstick ain't much.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:15.030
Whoever gets that neck is eating from now on.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.710
Commences here and runs plumb up to here.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:23.490
You ain't forgot what I said about Granny's jug of liquor.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:25.140
I ain't been to Granny's jug.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.240
You have your infert.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:27.900
Honest.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:28.890
Cross my heart.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.600
you see this chicken? Down by the Cement Pond. Cement Pond? Uncle Jed, that pond is the fanciest

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:42.580
thing you ever did see. Why, they are steps, so as the cattle can walk right down into

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:48.780
it and get a drink. And up one end, there's a lady standing there made out of rock. She's

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:56.170
a-pouring water out of a jar right into that Cement Pond. Well, that's how come that big

00:14:56.170 --> 00:15:00.000
old pink chicken to get away from me. Flew right over the top of that there rock, like

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.920
lady landed in that pond and swam like an otter, just lopping them big old pink wings

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.160
and hollered.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:09.990
What color did you say that chicken was?

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:10.990
It's pink.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:15.670
See, I told you that stuff was stunt your gross.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:18.630
Scrammy, I didn't touch a drop.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.640
You and me is going to the woodshed.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:22.590
I hope.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.350
Jethro, you swear to be telling the truth.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.050
So help me, Jefferson Davis.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:28.060
Mute!

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:30.000
Your hat off when you speak of the president.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.000
Well, you ain't president no more.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.750
Well, Yankee took my kitchen.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:42.670
Jethro, you and me is going back out there and corner that chicken.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:44.920
Granny, where are they going?

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.870
Ellie Mae, you sweep up out front and keep your eyes open for that Miss Hathaway.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:53.810
Count on her to take you in here and get you in the right kind of clothes. Come on.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.700
- I sure do thank you for thinking.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:16.860
- Clavin, you're a girl.

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:18.290
- Yeah.

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:21.210
- I'm Jane Hathaway, executive secretary to Mr. Drysdale.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.350
And you, I take it, are a domestic of some sort?

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.050
Cleaning girl, housemaid.

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.100
Just what are your duties with Mr. Clavin?

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:28.910
- Well, he sent me out here to sweep up,

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:30.000
but he said you'd take me in hand

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:31.800
and get him in the right kind of clothes and everything.

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:32.830
Indeed, I shall.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.830
We have a complete servant's wardrobe from chef to chauffeur.

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:36.650
Come with me.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:44.600
What in the name of Thomas Chippendale is this?

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.680
Does Mr. Clabber seem to have this disgraceful and unsightly mound of debris?

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:50.640
Oh, yes, ma'am.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.260
It's all his.

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:56.800
Oh, what charming antique.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.000
Just like he said, Jeff, you're right.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.700
Rock Lady pouring water into a cement pond.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:03.620
Yes, sir.

00:17:04.820 --> 00:17:07.100
And over there's where I seen that great big pink chicken.

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:10.160
Only thing is, it don't sound like a chicken.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.100
It makes a kind of hollering noise.

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:29.800
You'd make a hollering noise, too, if you was to lay egg like that.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.590
That's what I call an egg.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:36.780
You won't have to worry about food now.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:38.290
Well, she took this.

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:49.100
Stove don't draw worth nothing.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:51.570
Look at this egg.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:53.010
What's all the smoke?

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:54.480
Stove don't work.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.500
Probably just a stuffed-up flu.

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.000
Ain't got a flu, ain't even got a flu.

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:03.910
I'll get the shovel and the hole and rake it out of there.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.250
You keep this egg.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:17.950
Everything else out here.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:19.280
Froh solid.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:27.570
Hey, my dear, you look quite charming.

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:30.000
Well, I feel like a dad's blind foe.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.730
Just a moment.

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:34.310
Keep stopping. I'll be right back.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.490
Oh, that was my pa.

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:39.250
I take it he's a gardener.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:41.410
Oh, yes, ma'am. Pa just can't wait

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:42.270
to commence gardening.

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:45.740
However, as though I admire his enthusiasm,

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.700
I must permit him access through the main entrance.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:49.700
Now, tell me about

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:52.130
the rest of the staff. Is there a

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:53.650
chauffeur? A what?

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:55.640
Someone who does the driving.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.260
Oh, yes, ma'am. That's Jethro.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:00.000
Of course, I don't reckon he can

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.620
stay around very long, he's got to get back to Oxford and go to school.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:05.710
Oxford?

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:07.040
Who is that?

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:08.940
He attends Oxford University?

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.970
Well, he just calls it school.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.180
Paul went to school there, too.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:15.440
Oh, yes.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.320
Old school time.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.550
Great pride, those Oxford men.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:22.270
Sorry, I couldn't stop before.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:23.880
Granny was having trouble in the kitchen.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.350
Paul, do I have to wear this year's stuff that Miss Halsey...

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:29.960
No, no, there can be no appeal from Mr. Clavitt's orders.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:30.590
Thank you, ma'am.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:32.610
Glad to see you taking the firm hand.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:35.580
That's right pretty.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:37.180
Makes you look taller.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.200
She's got me walking on pegs.

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:42.230
Well, run in and show Granny.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:44.990
And get rid of those old clothes, dear.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.260
Granny, I presume, is the cook.

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:52.440
Yes, ma'am, but she ain't too happy about it right now.

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.000
I shall deal with her directly.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:55.800
Now, let's see.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.780
Maid, gardener, cook, chauffeur.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:19:59.860
Oh, uh...

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:06.570
i understand jethro is an oxford man you bet he is i'm uh quite anxious to meet him don't blame

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:14.050
you fine looking young fella single too and he's on the lookout for a girl i am only interested in

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:19.330
the intellectual rapport which i would naturally have with an oxford man i presume he went to eaton

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:30.000
as a boy if i know jethro he went to eaton the minute he was born yes of course i suppose his

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:37.630
think maybe it was his mom no matter at any rate he's an Eaton man that he is

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:43.540
matter of fact Jethro won the Eaton championship how marvelous what was he

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:53.310
champion of Eaton yes I know but what was it cricket no no it was crawdad I don't

00:20:53.310 --> 00:21:00.000
think even Jethro would eat crickets I was referring to the game where's that

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:07.030
i bet you granny you talk to miss hathaway and just why does cook need an axe i've hit this with

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:12.770
everything i could lay a hand to i even want it with an iron skill you are supposed to hit it

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:20.190
with a croquet mallet all right where is it i don't think i'll tell you there is a time for

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:26.710
work and a time for play now then what are we cooking for mr clavett well i don't know what

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:30.000
you're cooking him but if he wants any vittles for me somebody better

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.880
shoot a possum.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:32.880
Possum?

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.600
You got a better idea?

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:35.620
But of course.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:37.860
A nice big fluffy souffle.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.130
All right.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.130
You shoot it and you skin it.

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:43.540
You don't even know what a souffle is.

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:46.060
What kind of a cook are you?

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:51.820
I'm a cook with a stove that don't draw, food that's froze solid, chickens that can't

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:57.290
be caught, eggs that can't be broke, and a smart, alecky, city woman telling me my

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:58.300
business.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.000
That's what kind of a cook I am.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:01.870
Mind your tongue, little woman.

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:03.370
I can have your job.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.630
You sure can, and the sooner the better.

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:08.850
Jethro's coming, and just wait till you see what he's got.

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:09.930
Thank heaven.

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:12.510
Now I can have an intelligent conversation.

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:15.250
Look, Granny, I got that big jacket.

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:17.410
Well, I hope it ain't as tough as it's eight.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:19.030
But that is a flamingo.

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:21.820
Well, no, ma'am, that's my nephew, Jethro.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:23.580
Jethro, say hello to Miss Hathaway.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:25.000
I didn't mind him.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:28.610
I ain't the big dangest jacket you ever did say.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:30.000
You didn't go to school.

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:31.330
At ops?

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:32.610
Yes, ma'am.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:33.910
I'm in the fifth grade.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:36.230
Stop, dragon, and wrink that chicken's neck.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:37.170
Ellie, babe!

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:38.770
No, stop you, Marbella!

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.450
Don't arm one feather of that beautiful bird.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:44.270
I've taken just about as much as I'm going to take from you.

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:44.790
Yeah, Granny.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.590
Ellie, you get the fire going under the big kettle outdoors.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:49.290
Young lady, where is your maid's uniform?

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:50.510
I ain't going away.

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:51.790
Oh, yes, you are.

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:52.710
No, I ain't.

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:54.930
Oh, yes, you are if I must subdue you forcefully.

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.110
I wouldn't try to.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:22:57.650
Hey, don't be my dog!

00:22:58.270 --> 00:22:58.670
Ah!

00:22:59.070 --> 00:22:59.730
My dog!

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:01.250
I'm trying to tell you.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:01.870
Let me go.

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:03.190
Let me go.

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:06.280
Oh, really?

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:07.180
Let me go.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:08.640
Oh, here, here.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:10.920
Come on, let her up.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:11.740
Get off of me.

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:14.940
You guards!

00:23:15.590 --> 00:23:16.290
All of you!

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:18.230
Fired!

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:23.100
You are the most incompetent, insubordinate, belligerent group of domestics.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:24.360
It has ever been my miscursion.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:24.820
Miss Tathaway!

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:28.410
Ah, Chief, you're just in time to help me take disciplinary action.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:28.870
You're fired.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:30.000
I've already told them that.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.290
not them, you. These people

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:33.050
are the Clampets.

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:36.520
Clampets? Yes, that's right.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:38.900
Now go back to the office and pick up your severance

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:39.980
pay. You are through.

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:42.760
Now, Mr. Drysdale, hold on

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:44.560
a spell. I reckon there's been a kind of a

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:46.530
misunderstanding. But this little

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.470
lady's been a right big help

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:50.950
to us. Why, she fit in

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.570
just like one of the family.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:54.750
Matter of fact, her

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.950
and me is going out hunting. Ain't that

00:23:57.950 --> 00:24:00.000
right, Miss Hathaway? Yes, and

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:08.020
jethro you can take that pink chicken back out to the cement pond

00:24:08.740 --> 00:24:14.660
we're going out and shoot us a nice big fluffy souffle
